# Captive-portal probe hijack (travel-router role).
#
# OWNERSHIP (H03 exception): this file is owned and toggled exclusively by
# wan-select.sh — written in standalone mode (no WAN), removed when a WAN
# exists. install.sh NEVER renders it. dnsmasq is restarted by wan-select
# ONLY when this file's existence toggles (dnsmasq does not re-read dnsmasq.d
# drop-ins on SIGHUP, so add/remove requires a restart; content-identical
# ticks never restart).
#
# WHY STANDALONE ONLY: while a WAN exists, OS connectivity probes must pass
# through to the upstream (hotel) network so the hotel's own captive-portal
# login page reaches the client device. Hijacking probes during that phase
# would steal the portal login flow.
#
# local-ttl=10 keeps stale hijack answers in phone DNS caches short-lived so
# the "no internet" badge clears quickly after the drop-in is removed.
local-ttl=10
address=/captive.apple.com/__LAN_IP__
address=/connectivitycheck.gstatic.com/__LAN_IP__
address=/www.google.com/__LAN_IP__
address=/www.msftncsi.com/__LAN_IP__
address=/detectportal.firefox.com/__LAN_IP__
address=/nmcheck.gnome.org/__LAN_IP__
# Android DNS-value probe: answer the EXPECTED constant (131.107.255.255),
# never the Pi IP — a wrong value here makes some Androids flag "no internet"
# persistently. The HTTP probe (connectivitycheck.gstatic.com) is hijacked
# above; this one only needs to satisfy the DNS-level check.
address=/dns.msftncsi.com/131.107.255.255